# Figma Design File

This project's design-file tool. Owns the workspace where design work happens — a single shared design file at **{{DESIGN_FILE}}** (team **{{TEAM}}** / project **{{PROJECT}}**), organised by pages.

## Built on figma-use

This skill is an **overlay** on [figma-use](../figma-use/SKILL.md), Figma's official skill for driving the MCP server. `figma-use` owns the plugin-API mechanics. This skill owns the project's conventions — the page model, naming, the handoff signal.

**Whenever an operation here requires programmatic execution in Figma, follow `figma-use` for how to drive the API.** This skill never duplicates that material.

## Owned operations

- **create design page** — add a page for a screen or a feature (a set of related screens).
- **open design file** — open the project's pinned design file.
- **comment on design** — add Figma comments or sticky annotations.
- **mark frame ready for development** — apply Figma's Dev Mode "Ready for development" status to approved frames, in place.
- **archive frame** — move rejected explorations to the Archive page with a brief note.

## Access

All operations go through the **Figma MCP server**, driven via `figma-use`. Direct REST API calls or manual edits outside the MCP server are not permitted.

### Preflight (first operation of the session)

1. **Load figma-use.** Read [figma-use/SKILL.md](../figma-use/SKILL.md) before any `use_figma` call.
2. **Server health.** Verify the Figma MCP server is reachable and authenticated.
3. **Team / project access.** Verify the server can access team **{{TEAM}}** and project **{{PROJECT}}**.
4. **File access.** Verify file **{{DESIGN_FILE}}** is reachable.

## File organisation

**One shared design file for the project** — *not* one file per feature. All design work lives in this single file, at **{{DESIGN_FILE}}**, inside project **{{PROJECT}}**, alongside the Design System file. The file is organised by **pages**, not split across files.

### Pages

The unit of organisation is the **page**:

- **One page per screen**, or **one page per feature** (a set of related screens) — whichever fits the work. A small, self-contained screen gets its own page; a multi-screen feature or flow gets one page holding all its screens.
- Pages are named after the screen or feature they hold — descriptive, never `Page 1`. Sequential prefixes (`01_`, `02_`) help when there's a natural order.
- Keep one always-present **Cover** page (file name, overall status, last updated, an index of the design pages) and one **Archive** page (rejected explorations with brief notes on why).

### Within a page

- Use **Figma Sections** to group related frames (e.g., a flow, a set of states, side-by-side explorations) when a page holds more than a handful of frames.
- **Frames** are the individual screens and states.

## Conventions

### Frame and layer naming

- **Frames** are named descriptively — what they represent, not `Frame 42`. Sequential prefixes (`01_`, `02_`) help when a flow has a natural order.
- **Layers** describe their content or role (`Header`, `CTA Button`, `Error Message`).
- **Sections** group related frames with clear titles.

### Visual production

When producing high-fidelity designs:

- Use components and tokens from the published design system (no detached instances, no hardcoded values).
- Cover the interactive states relevant to the feature.
- When designs must **match a built interface**, derive sizing/spacing from the **implemented code** (the source of truth), and design only the screens that actually exist — don't invent unbuilt ones.
- If the components use **mode-varying tokens**, set the page's explicit variable **mode** (e.g., light/dark) **before** placing instances — otherwise they fall back to the collection default and render with the wrong values for that surface.
- **Verify each placed screen with the user**, not just your own render. A quick screenshot hides real defects (invisible borders, opacity dropped on publish, wrong variable mode); get per-screen sign-off.
- If the repo keeps a **Figma↔code component map** (e.g., `figma-code-map.json`; see `figma-design-system`), consult it so you reuse the mapped code component instead of reinventing — and cite the mapped code component in handoff so developers know exactly what to build or extend.

When the design system needs an addition, round-trip through `figma-design-system` — don't extend ad-hoc inside the design file.

### Handoff

- Approved frames are marked in place with Figma's Dev Mode **"Ready for development"** status — no separate handoff page; the status *is* the signal. Developers reference the marked frames directly.
- Layer names, component properties, and variables are clean and meaningful — Dev Mode is the developer's interface to the design.
- Annotations (Figma comments or sticky notes) explain interaction intent, state transitions, and accessibility requirements that aren't self-evident from the visual.

## Composition

When the `figma-design-system` skill is installed, the design file consumes its published library. Token or component changes round-trip through `figma-design-system`, then propagate here on republish.

When assembling a screen from **new** components, a cheap loop is: preview the screen with **local instances** in the design-system file for fast review → **publish once** → place the final screen here from the published library (`importComponentByKeyAsync`), then delete the preview. This avoids a publish cycle per iteration.
